.TH TERMINAL-AI 1 "2026-10-28" "terminal-ai 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
terminal-ai \- send prompts to a chat-completions backend and record responses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B terminal-ai
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ PROMPT ...]
.PP
.B terminal-ai
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.B <
.I prompt-file
.PP
.B terminal-ai
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B terminal-ai
sends a single user message to a chat-completions-compatible backend.
The message consists of command-line or standard-input text, followed
by any attached text files.
A fixed system message requests CommonMark-compliant responses.
.PP
The program supports three independently formatted output destinations:
standard output, an append-only transcript, and an optional artifact file.
Standard error carries diagnostics, interactive prompts, and response metadata.
.PP
Each invocation performs one request.
Existing transcripts are not read or sent as conversation history.
.B \-\-parent-id
affects recorded message identifiers only; it does not provide context
to the backend.
.PP
The request URL is the configured base URL, with trailing slashes removed,
followed by
.BR /v1/chat/completions .
The default endpoint is therefore
.BR https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions .
Only the first returned choice is used.
Responses are buffered rather than streamed.
.SS Important output behavior
In version 0.2.0 as supplied, user turns are not suppressed by default.
Both the composed user prompt and the AI response are written to standard
output, the transcript, and any configured artifact file.
This differs from the response-only behavior described in the embedded
help and source overview.
.PP
For response-only standard output and artifact output, specify:
.PP
.nf
-o NO_USER_STDOUT,NO_USER_ARTIFACT
.fi
.PP
Formatting presets such as
.B CONTENT_ONLY
remove decorations; they do not select which speaker is emitted.
.SH INPUT
If positional prompt arguments are present, they are joined with single
spaces and used as the base prompt.
Standard input is then ignored.
.PP
If no positional prompt arguments are present:
.IP \(bu 2
When standard input is a terminal, the program writes
.B "Enter prompt:"
to standard error and reads one line.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise, the program reads standard input to end-of-file.
.PP
Whitespace-only input read from standard input is rejected.
Positional prompt arguments do not receive the same emptiness check.
.PP
Each
.B \-\-input-file
is read as UTF-8 text and appended in command-line order using:
.PP
.nf
--- (@attachment: filename.ext) ---
<file contents>
.fi
.PP
Only the basename appears in the attachment label.
Attachments do not replace the base prompt: without positional text,
the program still reads standard input.
Files are sent as text within the user message, not as separate API uploads.
.SH OPTIONS
Options accepting values support both a separate argument and an equals
form, such as
.B "\-\-model NAME"
and
.BR "\-\-model=NAME" .
Recognized short value options also support equals forms such as
.BR "\-m=NAME" .
Concatenated short forms such as
.B \-mNAME
and grouped short options are not implemented.
.PP
For repeated scalar options, the last value wins.
Input-file options accumulate; output-control specifications are applied
in order.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help to standard output and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version
.B 0.2.0
to standard output and exit.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-model " " \fIMODEL\fR
Select the requested model.
Default:
.BR gpt-5.4 .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base-url " " \fIURL\fR
Set the API base URL.
Default:
.BR https://api.openai.com .
Do not include
.B /v1/chat/completions
in this value; the program appends it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-input-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Append a text attachment to the prompt.
May be repeated.
Missing or unreadable attachments are fatal errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-env-file " FILE"
Select an additional configuration file.
The process environment variable
.B TAI_ENV_FILE
takes precedence over this option.
A nonexistent explicitly selected file is a fatal error.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Skip the API request and use the literal response
.BR "<AI Response>" .
Input processing, transcript and artifact writes, timestamps, and message
ID generation still occur.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Write the raw backend response body to standard error before parsing it.
This may expose AI response content in diagnostics.
.TP
.BI \-\-transcript-dir " DIRECTORY"
Set the transcript directory.
A nondefault directory must already exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-output-dir " DIRECTORY"
Legacy alias for
.BR \-\-transcript-dir .
This does not set the artifact output path.
.TP
.BI \-\-transcript-file " FILE"
Set the transcript filename, joined to the transcript directory.
Default: the local date at startup followed by
.BR .md ,
for example
.BR 2026-10-28.md .
.TP
.B \-\-no-transcript
Disable transcript writes.
This does not disable standard output or artifact output.
Directory validation and default transcript directory creation still occur.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-output-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Write an additional artifact file.
The default is no artifact file.
When configured, output is appended unless
.B \-\-overwrite-output-file
is enabled.
Parent directories are not created.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite-output-file
Truncate the artifact file for each rendered, permitted turn.
With default speaker policy, the user turn is written first and then
replaced by the AI turn.
The operation is not an atomic replacement.
.TP
.BI \-\-parent-id " ID"
Use an explicit parent message ID for the recorded user turn.
The AI turn is parented to that user turn.
No earlier messages are loaded or transmitted.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output-control " " \fISPEC\fR
Apply comma-separated output-control tokens.
May be repeated.
See OUTPUT CONTROL.
.TP
.B \-\-
Stop option parsing.
All remaining arguments become prompt text.
.SH CONFIGURATION
For ordinary settings, precedence from highest to lowest is:
.IP 1. 3
Command-line option, where available.
.IP 2. 3
Process environment.
.IP 3. 3
Explicit configuration file selected by
.B TAI_ENV_FILE
or
.BR \-\-env-file .
.IP 4. 3
The application's
.B config.env
file.
.IP 5. 3
The application's legacy
.B .default.env
file.
.IP 6. 3
Compiled defaults.
.PP
Output-control tokens are applied after per-channel environment settings.
Boolean command-line switches force their setting on; they have no
corresponding force-off option.
.SS Exceptions
.B TAI_ENV_FILE
in the process environment overrides
.BR \-\-env-file ,
rather than the other way around.
It is not resolved from configuration-file contents.
.PP
.BR HOME ,
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ,
and
.B TAI_APP_NAME
are read only from the process environment.
Putting them in a configuration file does not change directory discovery.
.PP
Transcript directory selection is:
command line;
process
.BR TAI_TRANSCRIPT_DIR ;
process
.BR TAI_OUTPUT_DIR ;
file
.BR TAI_TRANSCRIPT_DIR ;
file
.BR TAI_OUTPUT_DIR ;
default.
.SS File syntax
Configuration files contain simple
.B KEY=VALUE
assignments.
Whitespace around keys and values is trimmed.
Surrounding single or double quotes are stripped.
Later assignments of the same key in a file replace earlier assignments.
.PP
Everything after the first
.B #
on each line is discarded, even inside quotes.
Blank and malformed lines are ignored.
Shell expansion, variable interpolation, escape processing, and
.B export
syntax are not supported.
Files are parsed into local maps; the process environment is not modified.
.PP
Missing default files are ignored.
Read failures, including an unreadable explicit file that exists,
silently produce an empty map.
A nonexistent explicit file is an error.
.SS Boolean values
Unless otherwise noted, true values are
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
.BR yes ,
and
.BR on ,
case-insensitively, with surrounding whitespace ignored.
Any other present value, including an empty value, is false.
.PP
Empty process or file values for ordinary string settings such as
model, base URL, API key, transcript filename, and speaker labels
are treated as unset.
Path settings do not consistently apply this rule; avoid empty paths.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The following application settings may be supplied through the process
environment or configuration files unless marked process-only.
.TP
.B OPENAI_API_KEY
Optional bearer token.
When set to a nonempty value, an
.B Authorization: Bearer
header is sent.
There is no command-line API-key option.
.TP
.B TAI_MODEL
Requested model.
Default:
.BR gpt-5.4 .
.TP
.B TAI_BASE_URL
API base URL.
Default:
.BR https://api.openai.com .
.TP
.B TAI_ENV_FILE
Explicit configuration file path; process-only.
Overrides
.BR \-\-env-file .
.TP
.B TAI_APP_NAME
Application directory name under the XDG configuration and data homes;
process-only.
Default:
.BR terminal-ai .
.TP
.B HOME
Home directory used for XDG fallbacks; process-only.
If absent, the program uses
.BR /tmp .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Configuration root; process-only.
Default:
.BR $HOME/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Data root; process-only.
Default:
.BR $HOME/.local/share .
.TP
.B TAI_TRANSCRIPT_DIR
Transcript directory.
Default:
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME/$TAI_APP_NAME/transcripts/default ,
with the fallbacks described above.
.TP
.B TAI_OUTPUT_DIR
Legacy alias for
.BR TAI_TRANSCRIPT_DIR .
Does not select an artifact file.
.TP
.B TAI_TRANSCRIPT_FILE
Transcript filename.
Default:
.BR YYYY-MM-DD.md .
.TP
.B TAI_NO_TRANSCRIPT
Disable transcript writes.
Default: false.
.TP
.B TAI_OUTPUT_FILE
Artifact output path.
Default: unset.
.TP
.B TAI_OVERWRITE_OUTPUT_FILE
Truncate the artifact for each emitted turn instead of appending.
Default: false.
.TP
.B TAI_SPEAKER_USER
Recorded user speaker label.
Default:
.BR USER .
.TP
.B TAI_SPEAKER_AI
Recorded AI speaker label.
Default: the nonempty model name reported by the backend, falling back
to the requested model.
.TP
.B TAI_STDERR_COLOR
Override ANSI color for runtime diagnostics.
Default: enabled when standard error is a terminal.
Unlike other booleans, recognized true words are case-sensitive:
.BR 1 ,
.BR true ,
.BR yes ,
and
.BR on .
Early argument/configuration errors and the interactive input prompt
use terminal detection before this setting is applied.
.SS Per-channel formatting variables
For each
.I CHANNEL
of
.BR STDOUT ,
.BR TRANSCRIPT ,
or
.BR ARTIFACT ,
the following variables are recognized:
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_ENABLE
Enable rendering for the channel.
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_HEADER
Emit a timestamp, speaker, and message-ID header.
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_INDENT
Prefix each body line with the indentation string.
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_SEP
Emit a trailing blank line and
.B ---
separator.
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_METADATA
Emit available response metadata inline.
This does not control standard-error metadata.
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_SPEAKER
Emit the speaker label on a separate line.
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_PROMPT
Emit the message body.
Despite the name, this controls both user and AI bodies.
.TP
.B TAI_CHANNEL_INDENT_STR
Indentation string.
Default: one space.
Used only when indentation is enabled.
.PP
Replace
.B CHANNEL
with the actual channel name, for example
.BR TAI_STDOUT_HEADER=1 .
There are no implemented
.B TAI_DRY_RUN
or
.B TAI_VERBOSE
settings.
.SH DEFAULTS
The rendering defaults are:
.PP
.nf
Setting       STDOUT    TRANSCRIPT    ARTIFACT
ENABLE        true      true          true
HEADER        false     true          false
INDENT        false     true          false
SEP           false     true          false
METADATA      false     false         false
SPEAKER       false     false         false
PROMPT        true      true          true
INDENT_STR    one space one space     one space
.fi
.PP
An enabled artifact renderer has no effect until an output file is selected.
No speaker suppression is enabled by default.
Transcript and artifact writes append by default.
Dry-run and verbose modes are disabled by default.
.SH OUTPUT CONTROL
Tokens are case-insensitive and surrounding whitespace is ignored.
Empty comma-separated tokens are ignored.
Unknown tokens are fatal errors.
.PP
A formatting token may end in
.BR _STDOUT ,
.BR _TRANSCRIPT ,
or
.B _ARTIFACT
to target one channel.
.B _FILE
is a legacy alias for
.BR _TRANSCRIPT ,
not for artifact output.
Without a suffix, a formatting token affects all three channels.
Standard error is never targeted by these tokens.
.PP
Tokens are applied left to right across all
.B \-o
options.
Later tokens override earlier formatting changes.
.SS Presets
.TP
.BR CONTENT_ONLY ", " PROMPT_ONLY
Enable body rendering and disable header, metadata, standalone speaker,
indentation, and separator.
Does not enable a disabled channel.
.TP
.B METADATA_ONLY
Enable metadata rendering and disable header, speaker, body, and separator.
Does not enable a disabled channel.
.TP
.B HEADER_ONLY
Enable header rendering and disable metadata, speaker, body, and separator.
Does not enable a disabled channel.
.TP
.B FULL
Enable the channel and all rendering flags.
.TP
.BR SILENT ", " DISABLE
Disable the channel.
.TP
.B ENABLE
Enable the channel without changing other flags.
.SS Individual flags
The following operations enable or disable individual flags:
.PP
.nf
Disable                      Enable
NO_HEADER, NO_HEADER_FLAG    ADD_HEADER, WITH_HEADER
NO_METADATA                  ADD_METADATA, WITH_METADATA
NO_PROMPT, NO_CONTENT        ADD_PROMPT, WITH_CONTENT
NO_INDENT                    ADD_INDENT, WITH_INDENT
NO_SEP, NO_SEPARATOR         ADD_SEP, WITH_SEP
NO_SPEAKER                   ADD_SPEAKER, WITH_SPEAKER
.fi
.PP
These operations do not independently enable a disabled channel.
.SS Speaker suppression
The following tokens suppress complete turns before rendering:
.PP
.nf
NO_USER_STDOUT       NO_AI_STDOUT
NO_USER_TRANSCRIPT   NO_AI_TRANSCRIPT
NO_USER_ARTIFACT     NO_AI_ARTIFACT
.fi
.PP
.B NO_USER_FILE
and
.B NO_AI_FILE
are aliases for transcript suppression.
There are no unsuffixed speaker-suppression tokens or inverse tokens
to restore suppressed speakers.
Suppression does not prevent the request from being made.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
When enabled, record components appear in this order:
header, standalone speaker, body, metadata, separator.
.PP
Headers have the form:
.PP
.nf
[timestamp, speaker, message_id]
.fi
.PP
Timestamps use local time from
.BR "date --rfc-3339=seconds" .
A nonindented body receives a final newline if it lacks one.
Indented bodies are processed line by line.
.PP
Metadata is generated only when the parsed response contains a usage block.
It is emitted to standard error regardless of inline metadata settings:
.PP
.nf
<!--{"model":"...","id":"...","usage":{"total":0,"prompt":0,"completion":0}}-->
.fi
.PP
Missing token counts become zero; missing model and ID fields become
empty strings.
Dry-run responses have no metadata.
.SS Message identifiers
A message ID has the form:
.PP
.nf
<self-hash>-<parent-hash>
.fi
.PP
The self hash is the first eight hexadecimal characters of SHA-256 over
the text
.IR timestamp,speaker .
The parent hash is the first eight characters of the parent message ID,
or the first eight hexadecimal characters of SHA-256 over
.B NULL
for a root.
.PP
IDs do not hash message content.
Turns with the same speaker and timestamp can have the same self hash;
these identifiers are not guaranteed unique.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/$TAI_APP_NAME/.default.env
Legacy configuration file, loaded first.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/$TAI_APP_NAME/config.env
Primary configuration file.
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/$TAI_APP_NAME/transcripts/default/YYYY-MM-DD.md
Default daily transcript.
Both user and AI records are appended unless suppressed.
.PP
With no XDG or application-name overrides, these paths are:
.PP
.nf
~/.config/terminal-ai/.default.env
~/.config/terminal-ai/config.env
~/.local/share/terminal-ai/transcripts/default/YYYY-MM-DD.md
.fi
.PP
The default transcript directory is created during normal argument
processing, even when transcripts are disabled or another directory
is selected.
A custom transcript directory must already exist.
Artifact parent directories and nested transcript filename directories
are not created.
.SH EXAMPLES
Send a prompt with response-only standard output:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai -o NO_USER_STDOUT "Explain Unix pipes."
.fi
.PP
Use piped text as the prompt:
.PP
.nf
printf '%s\\n' 'Summarize the purpose of POSIX.' |
    terminal-ai -o NO_USER_STDOUT
.fi
.PP
Attach multiple source files:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai -o NO_USER_STDOUT \\
    -i Cargo.toml -i src/main.rs \\
    "Review this program for correctness."
.fi
.PP
Write a response-only artifact, replacing its previous contents:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai -o NO_USER_STDOUT,NO_USER_ARTIFACT \\
    -O answer.md --overwrite-output-file \\
    "Write a short Markdown guide to shell quoting."
.fi
.PP
Write only an artifact, without standard-output or transcript content:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai --no-transcript \\
    -o SILENT_STDOUT,NO_USER_ARTIFACT \\
    -O answer.md --overwrite-output-file \\
    "Explain file descriptors."
.fi
.PP
Use a local compatible backend:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai -b http://localhost:8000 -m local-model \\
    -o NO_USER_STDOUT "Hello."
.fi
.PP
Test formatting without an API request or transcript writes:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai --dry-run --no-transcript \\
    -o NO_USER_STDOUT "Test prompt"
.fi
.PP
Keep a fully decorated transcript while piping only response content:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai -o NO_USER_STDOUT,FULL_TRANSCRIPT \\
    "List five useful shell commands." | head -n 5
.fi
.PP
Disable diagnostic color and capture diagnostics separately:
.PP
.nf
TAI_STDERR_COLOR=0 terminal-ai -o NO_USER_STDOUT \\
    "Explain redirection." >answer.md 2>diagnostics.log
.fi
.PP
Pass prompt text that begins with an option-like word:
.PP
.nf
terminal-ai -o NO_USER_STDOUT -- "--help is the text to explain"
.fi
.PP
Example configuration:
.PP
.nf
TAI_MODEL=gpt-5.4
TAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com
TAI_STDERR_COLOR=0
TAI_TRANSCRIPT_METADATA=1
TAI_ARTIFACT_INDENT=0
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal completion, help, version output, or a handled broken pipe.
A zero status does not guarantee a successful API request.
.TP
.B 1
Explicitly handled failures, including invalid options or output-control
tokens, missing required values, missing input files, invalid transcript
directories, missing stdin prompts, and file-write failures.
.TP
.B Other nonzero status
Unhandled failures may cause a Rust panic, normally exiting with status
101.
Examples include missing subprocess executables or failure to create
the default transcript directory.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
The following executables must be available through
.BR PATH :
.TP
.B curl
HTTP transport for nondry-run requests.
.TP
.B sha256sum
Message-ID derivation, including in dry-run mode.
.TP
.B date
Date and timestamp generation.
The implementation requires support for
.B \-\-rfc-3339=date
and
.BR \-\-rfc-3339=seconds ,
as provided by GNU date.
.PP
The program invokes
.B date
before processing help and version options.
A compatible date implementation is therefore required even for these
operations.
.SH SECURITY
Prompts and attachments are sent to the configured backend.
By default, both are also retained in the local transcript, and in any
configured append-mode artifact.
Use
.B \-\-no-transcript
and appropriate speaker-suppression options when retention is unwanted.
.PP
The API key and complete request body are passed to
.B curl
as command-line arguments.
They may be visible through process inspection to users permitted by
the operating system.
Configuration files, transcripts, and artifacts receive no special
permission hardening; normal filesystem permissions and the process
umask apply.
.PP
Verbose diagnostics include the raw response body.
Parse-failure diagnostics also include the raw body even without
.BR \-\-verbose .
Standard error must therefore not be assumed to exclude conversation
content.
.PP
The program sends any configured bearer token to the selected base URL.
Use trusted endpoints and HTTPS when transmitting sensitive data.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The implementation does not check curl's exit status or HTTP status.
Curl's captured standard error is not displayed.
Malformed or incompatible response JSON produces a diagnostic and an
empty AI body rather than a fatal error.
An empty choices array likewise produces a warning and an empty body.
.PP
There are no application-configured retries, request timeout, streaming,
conversation replay, configurable system prompt, or generation-parameter
options.
Response content must be a JSON string in
.BR choices[].message.content .
.PP
Output destinations are not transactional.
Records are written in standard-output, transcript, then artifact order.
A fatal write error or a handled standard-output broken pipe can prevent
later destinations from receiving the record.
Artifact overwrite mode truncates separately for each emitted turn.
.PP
Request JSON escaping is limited to backslash, quote, newline, carriage
return, and tab.
Other control characters can produce invalid request JSON.
Inline metadata strings are interpolated without JSON escaping and
should not be treated as reliably machine-parseable JSON for arbitrary
backend values.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR date (1),
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR man (1)
